# SYNOPSIS

```usage
which [-a] name ...
```

# DESCRIPTION

For each <name>, `which` looks in the directories of `$PATH`, in order, for an executable regular file of that name, and prints the first one's path.
A <name> containing `/` is checked as it is, and printed if it is executable.

: `-a`
  Print every match, not only the first.

`which` is sbase's, built into `sbase-box` with sbase's other commands and installed as `/boot/bin/posix/which`, a hard link to it.
In the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so POSIX programs find it as `/bin/which`.
There is no native `which`.

# SEE ALSO

env(1), rc(1); sbase's page is `third_party/sbase/which.1` in the repository

# DIAGNOSTICS

A <name> not found is reported as `which:` <name>`: not an external command`.
The exit status is 0 if every <name> was found, 1 if some were, and 2 if none were.
It is 3 if `$PATH` is not set (`which: $PATH is not set`); the usage message exits 1.

# BUGS

On VectraOS the C library's `faccessat` asks only whether a file exists, not whether it may be run, so every regular file of the name is printed, executable or not.

An empty element of `$PATH` is skipped, not taken as the current directory.

`which` is untested on VectraOS.
